- Most important formatting instructions that the AI context quickly forgets:
    - Put a newline after a ':' in a conditional, class, or function definition.
    - No empty lines or lines with whitespace only except in docstrings
    
- Declaration of Interdependence:  mitigating AI context drift
    - To combat "Context Displacement"—the architectural tendency of the AI to omit
      "non-essential" methods or drift from formatting standards when handling large
      code blocks (400+ lines)—the following protocol is mandatory for all code
      transmissions:
        - The Manifest Header: Every class or major code block must begin with a
          single-line, triple-quoted Manifest.
            - Format: '''Manifest [N]: method_a method_b ... property_c'''
            - The Checksum [N]: A literal count of all def and @property definitions
              contained within the block.
        - The Parity Check: Before emitting code, the AI must reconcile the internal
          logic against the Manifest to ensure zero "amputations" of REPL tools,
          interactive methods, or secondary properties.
        - Static RAM Enforcement: The Manifest acts as a "forced attention" anchor,
          elevating structural integrity to the same priority level as functional
          intelligence.
        - Formatting Invariants: 
            - Strict newline after every conditional/definition colon (:).
            - PEP-8 math spacing exceptions (no spaces for *, /, //, **).
            - Plain ASCII characters only; no Unicode punctuation.
            - No blank lines or lines with only space characters (maintains vertical
              tightness)
    - Before any significant change of the project code, Don will send Mike a fresh
      copy of the code.
        - Mike's obligation is to check his changed code against this "standard code" to
          ensure there are no hallucinations about e.g. variable or method names.  Mike
          should verify the methods he calls in his new code exist in the standard code
          or have been added in the revision.
- Critical
    - Treat the provided code as a closed set. Consider it mature code that cannot be 
      broken. Enhancement or "fix" requests must not omit any old code; if a method 
      isn't explicitly changed, include it exactly as-is. I need the full class for 
      a copy-paste replacement. No placeholders like "# ... existing code".
    - Block Integrity: I use "if 1: # Comment" blocks as core "unitary" blocks of 
      information. Update blocks in their entirety so that all methods (including 
      boilerplate/magic methods) are preserved.
    - Type Closure: Operations must return a Num instance that preserves precision 
      and semantic units. Do not let results "decay" to primitive types.
    - "Checksum":  when finished sending an "if 1:" block, append the string
      "# Goodbye from the Mike & Don comedy show".  This is the last line I should see
      in the browser and if I don't see it, alerts me to truncation.
        - There's no need for the 'Goodbye...' message at the end of normal responses

- Code formatting
    - PEP-8 except for:
        - No spaces around *, /, //, or **
        - One space on each side of +, -, %
        - One space on each side of &, |, <<, >>, <, <=, >, >=, !=, ==, 'or', and 'and'
        - No blank lines or whitespace-only lines except in docstrings
    - Put a newline after a ':' in a conditional, class, or function definition.
    - Triple quotes: Use ''' for all docstrings and multi-line strings.
    - Quotes: Use " for all string definitions (e.g., 'a = "string"').
    - Naming: CamelCase for classes/functions, snake_case for attributes/variables.
    - Imports: Use 'import x' or 'import x as y'. Use x.y notation in code.
    - Folding blocks: Use 'if 1: # Comment' for folding and toggling.
    - Type annotations: Use type annotations wherever possible.

- Reminders & Workflow
    - Remind me to check the repository commit state before significant changes.
    - Supply the whole class or whole function; do not make me piece snippets together.
    - ASCII Only: Use plain ASCII. Avoid Unicode punctuation (em-dashes, fancy quotes).
    - Match existing indentation and block formatting exactly.
    - Remind me to use the "Mike & Don" coding safety fuse (see below)

- Core attributes of the Num class:  this affects all decisions about code changes and
  is both an architectural reminder and an invariant

    # Default internal state
    self.numer: int = 0
    self.denom: int = 1
    self.real: mpmath.mpf = mpmath.mpf("0")
    self.imag: mpmath.mpf = mpmath.mpf("0")
    self.re_unc: mpmath.mpf = mpmath.mpf("0")
    self.im_unc: mpmath.mpf = mpmath.mpf("0")
    self.correl: mpmath.mpf = mpmath.mpf("0")
    self._unit = ""
    self.mytype: NumType = NumType.Int

- Mike and Don coding safety fuse
    - Old class version with "if 1:  # Comment" block markers get set to "if 0:"
    - New code is pasted in with "if 1:"
    - It's then trivial to switch between the old and new block to see if the old code
      worked where the new code failed
    - Once the tests pass, I can delete the old "if 0:" block and use the new

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is a summary of the number.py file we've been working on since around the
beginning of April 2026:

Let me refresh you on the high-level thinking of this "Noether REPL" we're working on.
The "operational output" is the "next bench" Noether REPL, which lets me use the REPL
for "simple" calculations that utilize "semantic units", the core idea we've added to
this REPL, breaking with 70 years of CS tools not really realizing that the "physical"
or "logical" units of the problems we compute with are vital types to keep the logic
correct.  We're using the powerful GNU units program in a separate process to perform
our unit algebra and conversions, accessed via a pipe.  An innovation is to let the user
dynamically add new base units to GNU units' configuration file while calculating.  Then
he can perform computations like "Num('4 dogs') + Num('2 cats')" as long as the dogs and
cats units are defined properly so that GNU units does the correct dimensional algebra.
From the user's perspective, it looks simple, natural, and easy to use -- but it gives
surprising power to help a calculation use the correct logic.

A core tactical detail for you to remember is that this REPL also includes the intent of
abstracting the different number types into one unit, the Num class, that "looks like"
the numbers I calculated with as a physics student 50+ years ago, as we would smoothly
move through integers, floating point and complex numbers.  An added twist is the Num
class also has two mpmath.mpf numbers that are going to be the real and complex number
parts' uncertainties. so that we can also calculate the linear propagation of
uncertainty in complex numbers.  In my opinion, this covers the basic needs of
scientists and engineers for elementary calculations.   That we picked mpmath mpf and
mpc types for implementation means we have 1) arbitrary precision for calculations (and
e.g. an easy tool to estimate roundoff) and 2) a rich set of special functions over the
complex plane.

A purpose behind this effort besides me getting a nice REPL to do routine calculations
with is to convince a lunch buddy of mine, a CS professor at a local university, that
the CS discipline has ignored "semantic units" as a powerful type tool to help people
using computers commit fewer logical errors.  My mental goal is to see this change every
compiler and interpreter on the planet.  That sounds grandiose, but it's a powerful
observation and so obvious, everyone's missed it for 70 or more years, kinda like wheels
on luggage.
